WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.860
Bat Bombs

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:07.200
Human beings are unique in the animal kingdeom&nbsp;
in many different ways. We have opposable thumbs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:13.500
which we evolved to play Xbox and operate complex&nbsp;
TV remotes, and our disproportionately large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:19.380
brains have allowed us to do incredible things&nbsp;
like send people to the moon and invent Fortnite.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:23.400
But perhaps the most striking&nbsp;
difference between human beings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:28.500
and our animal cousins is the way we’ve been&nbsp;
able to take control of the world around us.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:33.960
We’ve harnessed natural resources to power our&nbsp;
homes and chopped down trees to make room for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:39.240
the cities that contain them. We’ve dug deep&nbsp;
beneath the earth in search of precious metals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:44.700
and gemstones, and we domesticated plants&nbsp;
to feed our ever-growing numbers. But it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:49.320
isn’t just earth that we’ve shaped to suit our&nbsp;
needs, the same goes for other animals, too.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:55.320
Since the first time ancient man forcibly&nbsp;
parted a proto-chicken from its unborn child&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.320 --> 00:01:00.720
to make his scrambled eggs, we’ve happily&nbsp;
exploited earth’s other tenants for food,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:09.180
warmth, labour, entertainment, companionship,&nbsp;
and medical research - we’ve even roped them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:14.520
into our wars, and I don’t just mean&nbsp;
cavalry charges and carrier pigeons.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:21.120
Being the endlessly inventive - and&nbsp;
occasionally sadistic - species that we are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:27.360
we’ve forced animals to fight for us in ways&nbsp;
that are both fascinating and horrifying in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:34.620
equal measure, including bombs made of&nbsp;
bats, pigeon pilots, and explosive rats.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:40.620
But before we get into the ridiculous,&nbsp;
perhaps we’d better start with the equine.

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:50.760
Arguably the best known animal associated&nbsp;
with warfare is the horse, thought to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:55.980
been used as far back as 4000 BC. In the 6000&nbsp;
years since, we’ve used horses to carry us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.980 --> 00:03:02.760
into battle, transport our equipment, speed up&nbsp;
communications, and, in a pinch, fill our bellies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:07.380
Horses helped Genghis Khan conquer&nbsp;
half the world in the 13th century,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:12.720
and they were still being used by the&nbsp;
Polish army to fight off the Nazis in 1939,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:18.300
although admittedly that didn’t go too well.&nbsp;
Still, it’s no exaggeration to say that an armed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:23.820
man on horseback was the most devastating weapon&nbsp;
in existence for several millennia at least,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:28.740
with horses only being phased out during&nbsp;
the Second World War when tanks made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:35.460
them effectively obsolete. Do you think our trusty&nbsp;
warhorses upset by that turn of events? Nay.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:40.500
Elephants were also used as cavalry in&nbsp;
ancient warfare, first in India around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:45.120
the 6th century BC. Their main function&nbsp;
was to charge and break enemy lines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:49.920
sowing panic and fear along the way. As you&nbsp;
can probably imagine, they were incredibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:55.920
effective - a large unit of war elephants was&nbsp;
practically invincible… unless you happened to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:03.180
know their one fatal weakness, their achilles…&nbsp;
trunk, a counterweapon that, once deployed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:09.240
would cause even the bravest war elephant to drop&nbsp;
18 kilograms of excrement and run for the hills.

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:16.500
Any ideas what this secret weapon might&nbsp;
have been? If you guessed ‘war pig’ then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:22.140
1. You must have some highly unusual&nbsp;
reading habits, and 2. I’m impressed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:29.460
As odd as it may sound, pigs positively petrified&nbsp;
elephants, rendering them essentially useless.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:32.160
Still, that seems to have been&nbsp;
a fairly well kept secret,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:37.380
because elephants were still being used&nbsp;
as recently as 1987 by the Iraqi military.

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:42.360
Dogs have been employed during times of war&nbsp;
for hundreds of years to deliver messages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:50.520
take part in patrols and even carry slippers -&nbsp;
sorry, supplies - to the wounded. Over 50,000 dogs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:56.940
were used in WWI alone, thousands of which ended&nbsp;
up going to the great kennel in the sky. But we’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.940 --> 00:05:03.660
also conscripted our canine companions in some&nbsp;
more creative - and ultimately disastrous - uses.

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:09.000
One of the most brutal doggy duties, dreamed&nbsp;
up by the Soviets during the Second World War,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:15.660
was the anti-tank dog. As the name suggests,&nbsp;
anti tank dogs were trained to drop timed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:21.180
explosives underneath German tanks before&nbsp;
scampering to safety to admire the fireworks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:26.400
Simple though the idea was, carrying it&nbsp;
out in the theatre of war was another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:32.100
matter entirely.For one thing, the dogs were&nbsp;
often confused by wartime conditions and would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:37.740
run back to their handlers, bombs in tow,&nbsp;
with predictably disastrous consequences.

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:44.940
The original plan having failed spectacularly,&nbsp;
the Soviets came up with a new one - to use the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:51.240
dogs as unwitting Kamikaze fighters, armed&nbsp;
with explosives that would detonate as soon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:57.300
as they were released, killing the dog and&nbsp;
whatever happened to be nearby. If this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.300 --> 00:06:01.980
how man treats his best friends, I’d hate&nbsp;
to see what we’d do to our worst enemies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:09.660
Still, the dogs had the last laugh - kind of.&nbsp;
They’d naturally been trained on Soviet tanks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:17.580
but unlike the petrol guzzling tanks used by&nbsp;
the Germans, Soviet models ran on diesel. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:21.600
the heat and confusion of battle, the dogs&nbsp;
relied on their sense of smell to navigate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:28.980
and more often than not they headed straight&nbsp;
for the comforting, familiar smell of diesel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:36.360
Nobody’s quite sure exactly how many tanks were&nbsp;
destroyed by the Soviet anti tank dogs - friendly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:43.980
or otherwise - but the programme was quietly&nbsp;
mothballed in 1943 after many mortifying mishaps.

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:50.820
Like most Americans, dentist Lytle Adams was&nbsp;
incensed by the Japanese attack on Pearl Harbour,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:57.600
and he spent much of his spare time dreaming up&nbsp;
ways the USA could wreak her revenge. Unlike most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:02.580
Americans, though, Lytle had a direct connection&nbsp;
to the White House via Eleanor Roosevelt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:06.900
the first lady, which is how he managed to get&nbsp;
a letter through to the President of the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:12.420
States. The letter contained an audacious plan&nbsp;
that must have been just crazy enough to get the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:18.540
president’s backing, because it initiated one&nbsp;
of the most bizarre projects of the entire war.

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:28.860
This confounding contraption is a bat bomb. A 1.5&nbsp;
metre long bomb, containing 1040 compartments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:35.040
each holding a tiny amount of napalm,&nbsp;
and something far scarier… a small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:42.360
but mighty Mexican free-tailed bat.
The idea was that the “bomb” would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.360 --> 00:07:47.580
dropped from a plane, shedding its casing when&nbsp;
a parachute-controlled descent began at a height&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:55.020
of 1200 metres. Having served their time in bat&nbsp;
purgatory, the furry inmates would suddenly find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.020 --> 00:08:00.420
themselves free to go wherever they pleased,&nbsp;
taking their tiny napalm payloads with them.

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:07.680
As far as weapons go, the bat bomb&nbsp;
was - fittingly - bat shit insane…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:14.100
but it was also kind of ingenous. Japanese&nbsp;
houses are traditionally built of highly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.100 --> 00:08:20.160
combustible materials like wood and paper,&nbsp;
so fire was a major threat. And while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:25.500
regular incendiary bombs could have been used&nbsp;
instead, the bat bomb was far more devastating.

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:30.000
The bat’s payloads were on timers, and&nbsp;
once they went off they’d set fire to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:34.380
whatever was nearby. By that point, the bats&nbsp;
would have spread out over the entire city,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:40.260
and many of them would be happily roosting in&nbsp;
hard to reach - but highly flammable - hiding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.260 --> 00:08:45.180
places like the roofs of old buildings. By&nbsp;
the time anyone realised what was going on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:48.240
there would likely be hundreds of&nbsp;
fires raging all over the city.

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:56.400
Unlike the anti-tank dog disaster, the bat&nbsp;
bomb genuinely worked - a fact that was proven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:02.940
spectacularly when one was accidentally set off&nbsp;
in the middle of Carlsbad Army Airfield in New&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:08.040
Mexico during testing. Bats armed with napalm&nbsp;
were soon flying around all over the place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:14.760
setting fires and roosting in the most&nbsp;
awkward, hard to reach places… including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.760 --> 00:09:22.320
underneath a fully loaded fuel tank. The ensuing&nbsp;
explosion destroyed the entire testing area.

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:28.860
Subsquent, slightly more controlled tests&nbsp;
showed the bat bomb to be incredibly effective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:34.200
and the ingenious weapon may well have seen active&nbsp;
service had the programme been completed just a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:39.720
little bit earlier. In the end, the bat bomb&nbsp;
wasn’t declared ready for action until 1945,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:44.760
by which point the Americans had invented&nbsp;
a different kind of bomb - of the atomic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:51.600
variety. When a single doomsday device can level&nbsp;
an entire city in one explosion, painstakingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:57.780
glueing tiny parcels of napalm to a thousand&nbsp;
Mexican bats suddenly felt like a waste of time.

00:09:57.780 --> 00:10:03.780
Another American citizen keen to lend a hand&nbsp;
to the war effort was renowned behavioural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.780 --> 00:10:11.340
psychologist, inventor and patriot, Burrhus&nbsp;
Skinner, who, in 1943, aimed to solve the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:17.520
problem of missiles missing their targets. At the&nbsp;
time, electronic guidance was in its infancy and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:23.880
therefore both unreliable and expensive.&nbsp;
And that’s where Project Pigeon came in.

00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:30.060
Skinner had experience training pigeons&nbsp;
in his psychological research, and he had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.060 --> 00:10:35.760
a hunch that they’d be surprisingly well-suited&nbsp;
to war. Not only do pigeons have good eyesight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:41.880
but they remain remarkably unflappable even&nbsp;
in the most tumultuous of circumstances (as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.880 --> 00:10:46.320
anyone who's tried to shoo a pigeon away from&nbsp;
their sandwich in the park will already know).

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:52.440
Skiner planned to make use of these admirable&nbsp;
attributes by training our feathered friends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:59.520
as pilots. He designed a specially fitted missile&nbsp;
nose cone with a tiny cockpit big enough to house&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:05.700
up to 3 pigeons, each of which would be positioned&nbsp;
in front of a live video feed of whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:11.760
target needed blowing up. The pigeons had been&nbsp;
conditioned to peck whichever part of the screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:19.020
contained the target in return for a few seeds.&nbsp;
Depending on where they pecked, cables attached&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.020 --> 00:11:23.940
to their heads would feed information back to&nbsp;
the flight control system, which would make the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.940 --> 00:11:30.120
necessary adjustments automatically if the missile&nbsp;
started to go off course. Sadly, the projectiles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:36.300
weren’t fitted with ejector seats, so a pigeon&nbsp;
pilot’s first mission would also be its last.

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:40.740
The first demonstration of the&nbsp;
guidance system was successful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.740 --> 00:11:45.840
but the programme was terminated in&nbsp;
October 1944 as all focus moved to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:50.520
the Manhattan Project, and though it&nbsp;
was briefly resurrected after the war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.520 --> 00:11:57.000
improvements in electronic guidance eventually&nbsp;
saw the idea shelved for good. Had pigeon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:03.000
kind shared a collective consciousness, there&nbsp;
surely would have been a giant coo of relief.

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:08.940
The Americans’ ingenuity and inventiveness in&nbsp;
building bat bombs and pigeon guided missiles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:14.160
was really impressive, but they weren’t the&nbsp;
only ones blowing animals up in the name of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:19.920
miltary progress - the British also got in on&nbsp;
the act, though true to form, their attempts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:25.500
weren’t quite so grand as those across the&nbsp;
pond. The Brits, specifically the British&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:31.320
Special Operations Executive, made all manner of&nbsp;
ingenious devices during the Second World War,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.320 --> 00:12:39.060
but if you’re picturing Q handing 007 an elegantly&nbsp;
understated gerbil watch, you may be disappointed.

00:12:39.060 --> 00:12:45.540
While the Americans developed their blazing bat&nbsp;
bombs and put their pigeon pilots through their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.540 --> 00:12:53.880
paces, we Brits were busy coming up with…exploding&nbsp;
rats. Allow me to explain this highly intricate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.880 --> 00:13:01.740
device. Take a dead rat, slice it open and fill&nbsp;
its lifeless carcass with plastic explosives, then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.740 --> 00:13:08.220
sew it back up. Voila, you’ve just made your very&nbsp;
own exploding rat. Time to take over the world!

00:13:09.240 --> 00:13:13.980
OK, so the point of the exploding rat might&nbsp;
not be all that obvious at first. The fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.980 --> 00:13:19.380
it’s already dead means you still need a person&nbsp;
to take it to the place you want to blow up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:26.520
at which point you could just do away with the rat&nbsp;
carcass entirely. But here’s where the clever bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.520 --> 00:13:32.880
comes in - the explosive rats were intended to&nbsp;
be left in and around factories and other key&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.880 --> 00:13:40.200
production facilities, specifically those with&nbsp;
large industrial furnaces. Dead rats are a fairly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:45.360
common sight in those kinds of places, and when&nbsp;
found they’re disposed of. But you can’t just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.360 --> 00:13:50.340
dump a dead animal in the dustbin and forget&nbsp;
about it - not if you have a sense of smell.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.000 --> 00:13:55.740
It’s much better to deal with the problem once and&nbsp;
for all by shovelling the carcass into a furnace.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.740 --> 00:14:00.300
With a normal dead rat that solution works&nbsp;
just fine. But if your rigour-mortised rodent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.300 --> 00:14:06.120
happens to contain several grams of plastic&nbsp;
explosive, well. There goes your factory.&nbsp;

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:15.120
The idea was genius in its simplicity and it had&nbsp;
some success, though not as intended. The very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.120 --> 00:14:21.000
first batch of stinky and rather dangerous rodents&nbsp;
was intercepted by the Nazis before its occupants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:27.300
could reach their destinations. As a result, not&nbsp;
one single furry death bomb was ever thrown into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.300 --> 00:14:32.760
a furnace. But the discovery of a crate full&nbsp;
of exploding rats was enough to set Nazi high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.760 --> 00:14:39.420
command into meltdown as they imagined God knows&nbsp;
how many non-intercepted containers making their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.420 --> 00:14:45.600
way across the Third Reich. A colossal rat&nbsp;
hunt ensued, and so much time and money was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.600 --> 00:14:51.180
spent searching for exploding rats that the&nbsp;
operation was deemed a resounding success.

00:14:54.120 --> 00:14:59.220
Since the Second World War, animals in warfare&nbsp;
have come along in leaps and bounds. Today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.220 --> 00:15:05.820
there are rumours of programmes using&nbsp;
dolphins, seals and even spy sharks. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.820 --> 00:15:10.800
regardless of how sophisticated these&nbsp;
weapons become, they still rely on innocent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.800 --> 00:15:16.500
creatures being forcibly conscripted into the&nbsp;
unfortunate task of fighting our wars for us.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.220 --> 00:15:22.020
It doesn’t seem fair really. Someone&nbsp;
should call the military wing of the RSPCA.

00:15:22.020 --> 00:15:24.000
Thanks for watching.

